"Textbook: OpenStax <em>College Physics 2e</em>, §{#} (cohesion and adhesion in liquids: surface tension and capillary action); the same section in <em>College Physics for AP Courses 2e</em>. Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/college-physics-2e.",
"<strong>Used here:</strong> the <strong>Capillary tubes</strong> experiment (chosen above the picture) and its setups <strong>Water climbs thin tubes</strong>, <strong>Mercury sinks in glass</strong>, <strong>Water in a waxed tube</strong> and <strong>Glycerin: slow climb</strong>.",
"The picture shows three glass tubes standing in a dish of liquid. Their radii are \\(r\\), \\(2r\\) and \\(4r\\), where \\(r\\) is set by the <strong>Narrowest tube radius r</strong> slider in the set up card. Beside them is a graph of height against \\(1/r\\).",
"The strip above the picture holds <strong>Play</strong>, <strong>Reset</strong> (this experiment back to its start) and <strong>Time</strong>, the speed of the run, from {#}/{#} speed to {#} times faster. The clock shows the time since the liquid touched the tubes.",
"The set up card holds the <strong>Liquid</strong>, the <strong>Tube wall</strong>, the <strong>Narrowest tube radius r</strong> and <strong>Set the contact angle myself</strong>, which shows a <strong>Contact angle θ</strong> slider. Click a slider and use the arrow keys to set it exactly.",
"The Readouts list the liquid’s surface tension \\(\\gamma\\), the contact angle \\(\\theta\\), the density \\(\\rho\\) and the viscosity \\(\\eta\\), then for each tube its <strong>Height now</strong> and its <strong>Predicted h</strong>, and the time the narrowest tube takes to rise {#}% of the way.",
"<strong>Record reading</strong> (under Readings) stores one row for each of the three tubes: its radius, \\(1/r\\) and the height now. <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> sends the readings to this page’s Data table; <strong>Clear</strong> empties the Readings.",
"<strong>Blind: hide predictions and answers</strong>, in the Lab fold, hides the Predicted h readouts and the time to {#}%.",
"<strong>What the demo models.</strong> The height is \\(h = 2\\gamma\\cos\\theta/(\\rho g r)\\) with \\(g\\) = {#} m/s². Surface tensions are from Table {#} of the book, contact angles from Table {#}, densities from Table {#} and viscosities from Table {#} (water is taken as {#} kg/m³ at every temperature, as the book does). The liquid rises by viscous flow pushed by the surface’s pull less the column’s weight, so it slows as it nears its final height. Left out: the volume of the curved surface itself, the small fall of the level in the dish and the liquid’s inertia. The formula holds for tubes much narrower than the capillary length \\(\\sqrt{\\gamma/\\rho g}\\), {#} mm for water. Tube widths are drawn wider than life; heights are to scale.",
"Measure how high water climbs in glass tubes of different radii, show that the height goes as \\(1/r\\), and find the surface tension of water from the slope. Then find how the contact angle decides whether a liquid climbs or sinks, and measure the contact angle of mercury on glass.",
"<strong>Surface tension.</strong> A molecule inside a liquid is pulled equally on all sides by its neighbors. A molecule at the surface has neighbors only below and beside it, so it is pulled inward, and making more surface takes work. The surface therefore acts like a stretched sheet. The <strong>surface tension</strong> \\(\\gamma\\) is the force with which the surface pulls on each meter of an edge, in N/m. It is also the energy stored in each square meter of surface, since a newton per meter is a joule per square meter. Water at {#} °C has \\(\\gamma\\) = {#} N/m (§{#}, Table {#}); soap lowers it to about {#} N/m.",
"<strong>Contact angle.</strong> Where a liquid meets a solid wall, its surface meets the wall at a <strong>contact angle</strong> \\(\\theta\\), measured inside the liquid. If the molecules stick to the wall more strongly than to each other (adhesion stronger than cohesion), the liquid wets the wall and \\(\\theta\\) is less than {#}°: water on clean glass has \\(\\theta\\) = {#}°, and its surface curves up at the wall. If cohesion wins, \\(\\theta\\) is more than {#}° and the surface bulges up in the middle: mercury on glass has {#}°, water on paraffin wax {#}° (Table {#}).",
"<strong>Capillary rise.</strong> Stand a narrow tube of radius \\(r\\) in the liquid. The surface touches the inside of the tube all the way round its rim, a length \\(2\\pi r\\), and pulls along itself, at the angle \\(\\theta\\) to the wall. Its upward part is",
"\\[F = 2\\pi r\\,\\gamma\\cos\\theta.\\]",
"This force lifts a column of liquid of height \\(h\\) until the column’s weight, \\(mg = \\rho\\,(\\pi r^2 h)\\,g\\), balances it. Setting the two equal gives",
"\\[h = \\frac{2\\gamma\\cos\\theta}{\\rho g r}.\\]",
"The pull grows as the rim, \\(r\\), but the weight as the area, \\(r^2\\): half the radius, twice the height. When \\(\\theta\\) is more than {#}°, \\(\\cos\\theta\\) is negative, and the liquid in the tube is pushed down below the level outside (\\(h\\) negative). At {#}° nothing happens.",
"<strong>A straight line.</strong> For one liquid in one kind of tube, \\(h = (2\\gamma\\cos\\theta/\\rho g)\\times(1/r)\\), so a graph of \\(h\\) against \\(1/r\\) is a straight line through the origin with slope \\(s\\):",
"\\[s = \\frac{2\\gamma\\cos\\theta}{\\rho g}.\\]",
"With \\(\\theta\\) = {#}° for water on glass, \\(\\gamma = \\rho g s/2\\). Watch the units: with \\(h\\) in mm and \\(1/r\\) in mm⁻¹ the slope is in mm², and {#} mm² = {#}⁻⁶ m².",
"<strong>How fast it rises.</strong> The column climbs because the surface pulls it, but the liquid has to flow up a narrow tube, and its viscosity \\(\\eta\\) resists that flow (§{#}). The final height does not depend on \\(\\eta\\); the time to get there does. The rise is fast at first and slows as the column nears its final height, since the net push, the surface’s pull less the column’s weight, shrinks to zero.",
"Show that setting \\(2\\pi r\\gamma\\cos\\theta\\) equal to the column’s weight \\(\\rho\\pi r^2 h g\\) gives \\(h = 2\\gamma\\cos\\theta/(\\rho g r)\\). Then work out how high water at {#} °C (\\(\\gamma\\) = {#} N/m, \\(\\theta\\) = {#}°, \\(\\rho\\) = {#} kg/m³) climbs in glass tubes of radius {#} mm, {#} mm and {#} mm. Give each in mm.",
"What slope do you expect for the graph of \\(h\\) (mm) against \\(1/r\\) (mm⁻¹) for water in glass? Give it in mm². What should the intercept be?",
